  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Flown Dog",
    "overview": "The robot is flown, not charted. The screen is an EICAS glass cockpit (engine-indicating and crew-alerting system) rather than a KPI-tile dashboard: each of the Go2's twelve joint motors reads like an engine dial, the agent overwatch speaks through a crew-alerting stack, and every other system lives on a synoptic page that staff page through or that cycles by itself in booth mode. It is built to be read from about 3 m in a few seconds, and to reward a closer look at arm's length.\n\nThe chassis comes from Azimuth (`/Users/Raleigh/Development/azimuth/DESIGN.md`): near-black, cool-blue panels lifted by 1px borders and one inset top bevel, with no glow, gradient or glass. On top of it the booth adds two things. The first is EICAS cockpit grammar: master annunciators, severity-ranked messages, invalid-data dashes and green/amber/red dial arcs. The second is a **cloud accent** that is swapped at build time: Azure blue for Microsoft Ignite, AWS orange for re:Invent. Everything except the accent is shared between the two shows.\n\nThe whole cockpit is sized from one viewport unit, `--u` (`spacing.u`, about 11.9px on a 1920 screen), so a 1080p booth screen and a 4K one look the same from the aisle.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Near-black tonal panels; depth comes from 1px borders and a single 6% white top bevel.",
      "Status colours have fixed EICAS meanings: green nominal, amber caution, red warning, grey unknown.",
      "One cloud accent (Azure or AWS), used only for advisories, the selected page key, the attitude pointer and the brand word.",
      "Three typefaces with strict jobs: Chakra Petch uppercase for titles and alerts, IBM Plex Sans for sentences, JetBrains Mono tabular figures for every number.",
      "Instruments are drawn as inline SVG from data. There is no chart library and there are no pictorial icons.",
      "A live 3D twin of the Go2 is the hero. It follows the real joint angles at about 15 frames/s, its joint housings take on the status colour, and the view turns so a flagged joint is never hidden.",
      "The Overwatch column says each thing once: one agent line, terse severity-sorted messages, and lamps that only show values when something is off."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Cloud Swap Rule",
        "body": "The cloud brand lives only in `src/lib/theme.ts` and is chosen at build time with `NEXT_PUBLIC_THEME=aws|azure` (Azure is the default). `layout.tsx` writes `--accent`, `--accent-press` and `--accent-ink` onto `<html>`, and the \"Built on …\" text comes from the same theme. Components may only reference `var(--accent*)`. They never use an accent hex and never name a cloud in their copy.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Fixed Semantics Rule",
        "body": "Green, amber, red and grey mean nominal, caution, warning and unknown on every surface, including the 3D dog. The accent is never a status. Red appears only for a real warning.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Accent Rule",
        "body": "At any moment only one cloud accent is on screen, and it marks advisory, selection and pointer roles only. Do not use it for decoration.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Mono Number Rule",
        "body": "Every number that changes, every ID, every timestamp and every unit-bearing value is set in JetBrains Mono with tabular figures. Any text with digits in it gets the `.num` class.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Placard Rule",
        "body": "Chakra Petch is always uppercase, always tracked (0.06–0.08em), and never used for sentences.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The No-Glow Rule",
        "body": "There is no outer shadow, glow, gradient, blur or glass anywhere, including lamps and the architecture dialog. The dialog separates from the page with its dimmed backdrop (`rgba(4,6,8,0.72)`) and the panel bevel, not a drop shadow. Translucency is used for the leg quadrants (`surface-1` at 45% over the 3D stage, with a 70% border) and the live-twin badge (`surface-1` at 80%).",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
